al=121945+4374=126319 +145=126464 +4=126468.\n\nCheck if 126468 is a prime power.\n\nDivide by 2:63234\n\n÷2=31617\n\n÷3=10539\n\n÷3=3513\n\n÷3=1171. Check if 1171 is prime. Up to sqrt(1171)=34.2. After checking, 1171 is prime. Therefore, 126468=2^1×3^3×1171. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=30$:\n\nCompute $5(27000) +6(900) +5(30) +4=5(27000)=135000;6(900)=5400;5(30)=150; so total=135000+5400=140400 +150=140550 +4=140554.\n\nCheck if 140554 is a prime power.\n\nDivide by 2:70277. Check if 70277 is prime. Up to sqrt(70277)=264.9. After checking, it\'s found that 70277 is divisible by 7 (70277 ÷7=10041), 10041 ÷3=3347. 3347 is prime? Up to sqrt(3347)=57.8. Check primes up to 53. After checking, 3347 is prime. Therefore, 70277=7×3347. Therefore, 140554=2×7×3347. Not a prime power.\n\nBases up to, say, 30 haven\'t yielded any prime powers other than base7, which gave 2^11. Let\'s check higher bases. However, as the value of the polynomial $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4$ increases rapidly, let\'s see for base $b=31$:\n\nCompute $5(31)^3 +6(31)^2 +5(31) +4$.\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\n31^2 = 961, so 6*961=5766.\n\n31^3 = 29791, so 5*29791=148,955.\n\nThen, 5*31=155.\n\nThus, total is 148,955 +5766=149,531 +155=149,686 +4=149,690.\n\nCheck if 149,690 is a prime power.\n\nDivide by 2:74,845\n\nDivide by 5:14,969.\n\n14,969: check if it\'s a prime. sqrt(14,969)= approx 122.5. Check primes up to 127.\n\nAfter checking, 14,969 is 127^2=16129. So 14,969=1272. Therefore, 149,690=2×5×1272. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=32$:\n\n5*(32768) +6*(1024) +5*(32) +4= 5(32768)=163,840;6*(1024)=6,144;5*(32)=160; so total=163,840 +6,144=170, 984 +160=171,144 +4=171,148.\n\nCheck if 171,148 is a prime power. Divide by 2:85,574\n\nDivide by 2:42,787. \n\nCheck if 42,787 is a prime. Up to sqrt(42,787)=206.9. After checking, it\'s not. So 171,148 is 22×42,787, not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=33$:\n\n5*(35,937) +6*(1,089) +5*(33) +4=5*35,937=179,685;6*1,089=6,534;5*33=165; total=179,685+6,534=186,219+165=186,384+4=186,388.\n\nCheck if 186,388 is a prime power. Divide by 2:93,194\n\nDivide by 2:46,597. Check if 46,597 is prime. Up to sqrt(46,597)=215.9. After checking, it\'s found that 46,597=13*3584.15, not prime. So nope.\n\nBase $b=34$:\n\n5*(39,304) +6*(1,156) +5*(34) +4=5*39,304=196,520;6*1,156=6,936;5*34=170; total=196,520+6,936=203,456+170=203,626+4=203,630.\n\nCheck if 203,630 is a prime power. Divide by 2:101,815\n\nDivide by5:20,363.\n\nCheck if 20,363 is prime. Up to sqrt(20,363)≈142.6. After checking, it\'s not prime. So not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=35$:\n\n5*(42,875) +6*(1,225) +5*(35) +4=5*42,875=214,375;6*1,225=7,350;5*35=175; total=214,375+7,350=221,725+175=221,900+4=221,904.\n\nCheck if 221,904 is a prime power. Divide by 2:110,952\n\nDivide by2=55,476\n\nDivide by2=27,738\n\nDivide by2=13,869\n\nDivide by3=4,623\n\nDivide by3=1,541.\n\n1,541 is prime? Up to sqrt(1,541)=39.26. Check primes up to 43. After checking, 1,541 is prime. Therefore, 221,904=2^3×3^2×1,541. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=36$:\n\n5*(46,656) +6*(1,296) +5*(36) +4=5*46,656=233,280;6*1,296=7,776;5*36=180; total=233,280+7,776=241,056+180=241,236+4=241,240.\n\nCheck if 241,240 is a prime power. Divide by 2:120,620\n\nDivide by2=60,310\n\nDivide by2=30,155\n\nDivide by5=6,031.\n\nCheck if 6,031 is prime. Up to sqrt(6,031)=77.99. Checking primes up to 73. After checking, 6,031 is prime. Therefore, 241,240=2^3×5×6,031. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=37$:\n\n5*(50,653) +6*(1,369) +5*(37) +4=5*50,653=253,265;6*1,369=8,214;5*37=185; total=253,265+8,214=261,479+185=261,664 +4=261,668.\n\nCheck if 261,668 is a prime power. Divide by 2:130,834\n\nDivide by2=65,417.\n\nCheck if 65,417 is prime. Up to sqrt(65,417)=255.8. Check primes up to 257. After checking, it\'s found that 65,417 is divisible by 13 (65,417 ÷13=5,031.307... Not integer. Next prime 17: 65,417 ÷17=3,848. 17×3848=65,416, remainder 1. So not. Next 19: 65,417 ÷19=3,442.947... No. 23: 65,417 ÷23=2,844.173... No. 29: 65,417 ÷29=2,255.758... No. 31: 65,417 ÷31=2,113.419... No. 37: 65,417 ÷37=1,765.300... No. 41: 65,417 ÷41=1,600.17... No. 43: 65,417 ÷43=1,519.186... No. 47: 65,417 ÷47=1,389.425... No. 53: 65,417 ÷53=1,234.396... No. 59: 65,417 ÷59=1,108.135... No. 61: 65,417 ÷61=1,075.541... No. 67: 65,417 ÷67=976.955... No. 71: 65,417 ÷71=921. So 65,417=71×921. Check 921: 921 ÷3=307, which is prime. Therefore, 65,417=71×3×307. Therefore, 261,668=2^2×71×3×307. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=38$:\n\n5*(54,872) +6*(1,444) +5*(38) +4=5*54,872=274,360;6*1,444=8,664;5*38=190; total=274,360+8,664=283,024+190=283,214 +4=283,218.\n\nCheck if 283,218 is a prime power. Divide by 2:141,609.\n\n141,609: check if prime. sqrt(141,609)=376.3. Check primes up to 373. After checking, it\'s not prime (divisible by 13, for example). So, 283,218 is not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=39$:\n\n5*(59,319) +6*(1,521) +5*(39) +4=5*59,319=296,595;6*1,521=9,126;5*39=195; total=296,595+9,126=305,721+195=305,916 +4=305,920.\n\nCheck if 305,920 is a prime power. Divide by 2:152,960\n\nDivide by2=76,480\n\nDivide by2=38,240\n\nDivide by2=19,120\n\nDivide by2=9,560\n\nDivide by2=4,780\n\nDivide by2=2,390\n\nDivide by2=1,195\n\nDivide by5=239.\n\n239 is prime. Therefore, 305,920=2^5×5×239. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=40$:\n\n5*(51,200) +6*(1,440) +5*(40) +4=5*51,200=256,000;6*1,440=8,640;5*40=200; total=256,000+8,640=264,640 +200=264,840 +4=264,844.\n\nCheck if 264,844 is a prime power. Divide by 2:132,422\n\nDivide by2=66,211.\n\nCheck if 66,211 is prime. Up to sqrt(66,211)=257.3. Check primes up to 263. After checking, 66,211 is found to be 263×251. Therefore, 264,844=2^2×263×251. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=41$:\n\n5*(54,867) +6*(1,681) +5*(41) +4=5*54,867=274,335;6*1,681=10,086;5*41=205; total=274,335+10,086=284,421 +205=284,626 +4=284,630.\n\nCheck if 284,630 is a prime power. Divide by 2:142,315\n\nDivide by5:28,463.\n\nCheck if 28,463 is prime. Up to sqrt(28,463)=168.7. Check primes up to 167. After checking, it\'s found that 28,463 is divisible by 13 (28,463 ÷13=2,189.461... Not integer). 17? 28,463 ÷17=1,674.294... No. 19: 28,463 ÷19=1,497.947... No. 23: 28,463 ÷23=1,237.478... No. 29: 28,463 ÷29=981.137... No. 31: 28,463 ÷31=918.129... No. 37: 28,463 ÷37=769.297... No. 41: 28,463 ÷41= 693.2. No. 43: 28,463 ÷43=659.558... No. 47: 28,463 ÷47=605.744... No. 53: 28,463 ÷53=535.113... No. 59: 28,463 ÷59=482.813... No. 61: 28,463 ÷61=466.5. 61×466=28,426, so remainder 37. Not. 67: 28,463 ÷67=422.5. No. 71: 28,463 ÷71=399.5... No. 73: 28,463 ÷73=389.5... No. 79: 28,463 ÷79=359.5... No. 83: 28,463 ÷83=341. So 28,463=83×341. Check 341: 341 ÷11=31, which is prime. Therefore, 28,463=83×11×31. Thus, 284,630=2×5×83×11×31. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=42$:\n\n5*(58,322) +6*(1,764) +5*(42) +4=5*58,322=291,610;6*1,764=10,584;5*42=210; total=291,610+10,584=302,194 +210=302,404 +4=302,408.\n\nCheck if 302,408 is a prime power. Divide by 2:151,204\n\nDivide by2=75,602\n\nDivide by2=37,801.\n\nCheck if 37,801 is prime. Up to sqrt(37,801)=194.4. Check primes up to 193. After checking, 37,801 is found to be 13×29×107. Therefore, 302,408=2^3×13×29×107. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=43$:\n\nCompute 5*(63,156) +6*(1,849) +5*(43) +4=5*63,156=315,780;6*1,849=11,094;5*43=215; total=315,780+11,094=326,874 +215=327,089 +4=327,093.\n\nCheck if 327,093 is a prime power. Divide by 3:109,031\n\nCheck if 109,031 is prime. Up to sqrt(109,031)=330.2. After checking, it\'s found that 109,031 is divisible by 13 (109,031 ÷13=8,387). 8,387 is 13×645.15, so not prime. Therefore, 327,093=3×13×8387. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=44$:\n\nCompute 5*(68,147) +6*(1,936) +5*(44) +4=5*68,147=340,735;6*1,936=11,616;5*44=220; total=340,735+11,616=352,351 +220=352,571 +4=352,575.\n\nCheck if 352,575 is a prime power. Divide by 3:117,525\n\nDivide by3=39,175\n\nDivide by5=7,835\n\nDivide by5=1,567.\n\nCheck if 1,567 is prime. Up to sqrt(1,567)=39.6. Check primes up to 41. After checking, 1,567 is prime. Thus, 352,575=3^2×5^2×1,567. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=45$:\n\nCompute 5*(72,900) +6*(2,025) +5*(45) +4=5*72,900=364,500;6*2,025=12,150;5*45=225; total=364,500+12,150=376,650 +225=376,875 +4=376,879.\n\nCheck if 376,879 is a prime power. Divide by 7:53,839.571... Not integer. Divide by 3: 376,879 ÷3=125,626.333... Not. Divide by 5: 75,375.8. Not. Divide by 13:29,067.538... Not. By 17:22,169.235... Not. By 19:19,835.789... Not. By 23:16,381.739... Not. By29:13,000. 29*13,000=377,000, so remainder -123. Not. By31:376,879 ÷31=12193.193... Not. Maybe check if prime. Up to sqrt(376,879)=613.9. After checking, it\'s prime. Therefore, 376,879 is prime. Therefore, 376,879 is prime, so not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=46$:\n\nCompute 5*(78,904) +6*(2,116) +5*(46) +4=5*78,904=394,520;6*2,116=12,696;5*46=230; total=394,520+12,696=407,216 +230=407,446 +4=407,450.\n\nCheck if 407,450 is a prime power. Divide by 2:203,725\n\nDivide by5=40,745\n\nDivide by5=8,149.\n\nCheck if 8,149 is prime. Up to sqrt(8,149)=90.2. Check primes up to 89. After checking, 8,149 is prime. Therefore, 407,450=2×5^2×8,149. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=47$:\n\nCompute 5*(83,297) +6*(2,209) +5*(47) +4=5*83,297=416,485;6*2,209=13,254;5*47=235; total=416,485+13,254=429,739 +235=429,974 +4=429,978.\n\nCheck if 429,978 is a prime power. Divide by 2:214,989\n\nDivide by3:71,663\n\nCheck if 71,663 is prime. Up to sqrt(71,663)=267.7. Check primes up to 263. After checking, 71,663 is prime. Thus, 429,978=2×3×267, not. Wait: 214,989=3×71,663. Therefore, 429,978=2×3×71,663. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=48$:\n\nCompute 5*(88,473) +6*(2,304) +5*(48) +4=5*88,473=442,365;6*2,304=13,824;5*48=240; total=442,365+13,824=456,189+240=456,429 +4=456,433.\n\nCheck if 456,433 is a prime. Up to sqrt(456,433)=675.6. Check primes up to 673. After checking, 456,433 is found to be prime. Therefore, not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=49$:\n\nCompute 5*(94,089) +6*(2,401) +5*(49) +4=5*94,089=470,445;6*2,401=14,406;5*49=245; total=470,445+14,406=484,851+245=485,096 +4=485,100.\n\nCheck if 485,100 is a prime power. Divide by 2:242,550\n\nDivide by2=121,275\n\nDivide by5=24,255\n\nDivide by5=4,851.\n\n4,851: divide by3=1,617; divide by3=539. 539 ÷7=77; 77=7×11. Therefore, 4,851=3^2×7×11×13? Wait: 3*3*7*11*13=?\n\n3*3=9; 9*7=63; 63*11=702; 702*13=9126. So no. Actually, factorizing 4,851:\n\n4,851 ÷3=1,617\n\n1,617 ÷3=539\n\n539 ÷7=77\n\n77 ÷7=11\n\nSo, 4,851=3^2×7^2×11. Thus, 485,100=2^2×3^4×5^2×7^2×11. Not a prime power.\n\nBase $b=50$:\n\nCompute 5*(125,000) +6*(2,500) +5*(50) +4=5*125,000=625,000;6*2,500=15,000;5*50=250; total=625,000+15,000=640,000 +250=640,250 +4=640,254.\n\nCheck if 640,254 is a prime power. Divide by 2:320,127\n\nDivide by3:106,709\n\nCheck if 106,709 is prime. Up to sqrt(106,709)=326.7. Check primes up to 323. After checking, 106,709 is found to be composite (for example, 106,709 ÷7=15,244.142... Not integer. 106,709 ÷11=9,700.818... Not. Maybe 106,709 ÷13=8,208.384... Not. Continue). So 323: 323*331=106,723, which is over. So 106,709 is prime? Then 640,254=2×3×106,709. Not a prime power.\n\nHmm, considering that bases from 7 to at least 50 haven\'t produced any prime powers other than 2048 (which was $2^{11}$ at base7). Considering that prime powers become less frequent as numbers get larger, and given that the expression $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4$ grows as $5b^3$, the values for larger $b$ will be astronomically large, making it impractical to check each one. However, perhaps there are no other solutions. But the problem states "For which bases $b>6$", so we need to check if base7 is the only one. But maybe there\'s a base higher than 50 where this expression is a prime power. But it\'s unclear. Alternatively, perhaps we need to think of other approaches.\n\nAlternatively, instead of testing bases sequentially, perhaps check if for some prime powers $p^k$, solve for $b$ in the equation $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4 =p^k$. However, solving for $b$ is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, factor the polynomial $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4$. Let\'s attempt to factor it.\n\nLet’s check if the polynomial can be factored:\n\n$5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4$.\n\nPossible factors could be of the form $(ab + c)(5b^2 + db + e)$. Let\'s attempt to factor it.\n\nAlternatively, use rational root theorem. Possible rational roots are factors of 4 (constant term) over factors of 5 (leading coefficient). So possible roots: ±1, ±2, ±4, ±1/5, ±2/5, ±4/5.\n\nTesting these in the polynomial:\n\nAt b=1: 5+6+5+4=10 ≠0\n\nAt b=2: 40 +24 +10 +4=78 ≠0\n\nAt b=3: 135 + 162 +15 +4=316≠0\n\nAt b=4: 320 + 96 +20 +4=436≠0\n\nAt b= -1: -5 +6 -5 +4=0 So, (b +1) is a factor.\n\nTherefore, factor as (5b2 + some terms)(b +1). Then perform polynomial division or use synthesis division.\n\nDivide $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4$ by $(b +1)$.\n\nUsing synthetic division:\n\nCoefficient array: 5,6,5,4 | -1\n\n      5* (-1) = -5\n\n       6 + (-5)=1\n\n1 + 5*(-1)= -4\n\n-4 + 4 =0\n\nThe remainder is 0. Thus, the polynomial factors as (b +1)(5b2 +1).\n\nThus, $5b^3 +6b^2 +5b +4=(b +1)(5b2 +1)$.\n\nTherefore, for the original expression to be a prime power, either $(b +1)$ or $(5b2 +1)$ must be a prime power. Since $b$ is a base greater than 6, $b +1$ is a number between 7 and 8. The only prime power in that range is 7. Therefore, if $b +1=7$, then $b=6$, which is not greater than 6. Therefore, discard $b +1=7$ (so $b=6$), but since $b>6$, we need to check if $5b2 +1$ is a prime power.\n\nThus, $5b2 +1 = p^k$ where $p$ is prime. So solve for $b >6: 5b2 +1 = p^k$.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding bases $b>6$ such that $5b2 +1$ is a prime power.\n\nTherefore, by factoring the original polynomial, we can narrow down the problem to solving $5b2 +1 = p^k$.\n\nTherefore, let\'s set $5b2 +1 = p^k$, with $b >6$ and $p$ prime, $k \\geq 1$.\n\nWe need to find all integers $b >6$ such that $5b2 +1$ is a prime power.\n\nThis is easier to manage than the original problem.\n\nSo now, our task is to find all integers $b>6$ where $5b2 +1$ is a prime power. Since $5b2 +1$ must be a prime power, let\'s consider that $5b2 +1 = p^k$, where $p$ is prime and $k \\geq 1$.\n\nFirst, note that if $p=2$, check whether $5b2 +1$ is a power of 2.\n\nIf $5b2 +1$ is even, then $5b2$ must be odd, which would mean that $5b2$ is odd, so $b2$ is odd, implying $b$ is odd. Then $5b2$ is congruent to 5*(1) mod 2=1 mod 2, so $5b2 +1≡0 mod 2$, so if $5b2 +1$ is a power of 2 and even, it must be at least 4. Therefore, $5b2 +1=2^m$, where $m≥2$.\n\nSo if $5b2 +1$ is a power of 2, subtract 1: $5b2=2^m -1$. So, $5b2=2^m -1$. Then $b2=(2^m -1)/5$. Therefore, $(2^m -1)$ must be divisible by 5.\n\nCheck when $2^m ≡1 mod5$. The multiplicative order of 2 mod5 is 4, since 2^1=2, 2^2=4, 2^3=3, 2^4=1 mod5. Thus, $2^m ≡1 mod5$ when m divisible by4. Therefore, m=4,8,12,...\n\nTherefore, for m=4: $2^4 -1=16-1=15$, so $b2=15/5=3→ b=√3$, not integer.\n\nm=8: $2^8-1=256-1=255$, so $b2=255/5=51→b=√51≈7.14$, not integer.\n\nm=12: $2^{12}-1=4096-1=4095$, $4095/5=819$. $b2=819→b=√819≈28.62$, not integer.\n\nm=16: $2^{16}-1=65536-1=65535$, $65535/5=13107$. $√13107≈114.9$, and so on. These b are not integers, so no solution with p=2.\n\nTherefore, there are no solutions where $5b2 +1$ is a power of 2 with b>6.\n\nNext, check if $5b2 +1$ is an odd prime power. Then $5b2$ is even (since $5b2 +1$ is odd, $5b2$ must be even, so $b$ must be even).\n\nLet’s write b=2n where n is integer > 3. (Since b>6, n>3). Then:\n\n$5b2 +1=5*(4n2) +1=20n2 +1$.\n\nTherefore, we need $20n2 +1 = p^k$, with p an odd prime, k≥1.\n\nWe need to find integers n>3 such that $20n2 +1 = p^k$.\n\nLet\'s check n=4 (b=8):\n\n20*16 +1=321. 321 factors as 3*107. Not a prime power.\n\nn=5 (b=10): 20*25 +1=501=3*167. Not prime power.\n\nn=6 (b=12):20*36 +1=721. 721÷13=55.46, ÷53=13.43, ÷17=42.41... Not prime.\n\nn=7 (b=14):20*49 +1=981=9×109. Not prime power.\n\nn=8 (b=16):20*64 +1=1281. 1281 ÷3=427. 427 ÷7=61. 427=7×61. Thus, 1281=3×7×61. Not prime power.\n\nn=9 (b=18):20*81 +1=1621. Check if prime. Up to sqrt(1621)=40.26. Check primes up to 43. After checking, 1621 is prime. Therefore, 20n2 +1=1621=401×40. Wait, 1621 is prime. So 1621 is prime, so yes, prime power. Thus, p=1621, k=1. But wait, the original expression is $20n2 +1= p^k$. If p=401, since 401^1=401, 401*401=160,801. 1621≠160,801. Therefore, mistake here. 20n2 +1=1621, which would require n2=(1621 -1)/20=1620/20=81. So n=9, which gives b=18. Thus, in this case, 20n2 +1=20*81+1=1621=401×1, which is a prime power. Therefore, p=401, k=1. But 401 is prime. Therefore, this is a prime power.\n\nTherefore, base b=18: 20*182 +1=20*324 +1=6480 +1=6481. Wait, no. Wait, previously for n=9 (b=18), 20n2 +1=20*81 +1=1621. 1621 is a prime number. Therefore, in this case, $5b2 +1=1621=401 ×1$, so it\'s a prime power (p=1621, k=1). Therefore, base18 is a solution.\n\nTherefore, base b=18: 5654 in base18 converts to 1621 in decimal, which is a prime power (prime).\n\nCheck if this conversion is accurate:\n\nIn base18, 5654 stands for:\n\n5*183 +6*182 +5*18 +4\n\n=5*5832 +6*324 +5*18 +4\n\n=29,160 +1,944 +90 +4 =29,160+1,944=31,104+90=31,200+4=31,204. Wait, but earlier when b=18, the conversion yielded 203,630. Confusion here. Wait, this discrepancy suggests an error. Wait, when we set b=18, then the digits are 5,6,5,4. So:\n\n5*18^3 +6*18^2 +5*18 +4.\n\nCompute 18^3=5832, so 5*5832=29,160.\n\n18^2=324, 6*324=1,944.\n\n18*5=90, then 5*18=90 -> wait, no: 5*18=90, added with 5*18? Wait, the number is 5 6 5 4 in base18, so:\n\n5*183 +6*182 +5*18 +4=5*5832 +6*32'}]}
